(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-anatomy-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 4 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"On which side of the forearm is the ulna located in anatomical position?","choices":["The lateral side","The anterior side only","The medial side","The posterior side only"],"answer":2,"exp":"In anatomical position (palm forward), the ulna is the medial forearm bone, lying on the little-finger side. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"In the mnemonic 'Sally left the party to take Cathy home,' which carpal does the first word ('Sally') represent?","choices":["The pisiform","The hamate","The trapezoid","The scaphoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the mnemonic the first word, 'Sally,' stands for the scaphoid, the first bone of the proximal row on the lateral side. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Where is the costal tuberosity of the clavicle located?","choices":["Near the sternal (medial) end","Near the acromial (lateral) end","On the superior surface only","At the midpoint of the shaft"],"answer":0,"exp":"The costal tuberosity is located near the sternal (medial) end of the clavicle, along with the sternal facet. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which structure is a large notch on the posterior ischium, providing passage for the sciatic nerve?","choices":["Lesser sciatic notch","Greater sciatic notch","Ischial tuberosity","Obturator foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The greater sciatic notch is the large posterior notch through which the sciatic nerve passes. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which two femoral condyles are found at the distal end and articulate with the tibia?","choices":["Head and neck","Medial and lateral condyles","Greater and lesser trochanters","Medial and lateral epicondyles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial and lateral condyles are the distal femoral structures that articulate with the tibia at the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"The proximal head of the radius articulates with the capitulum and is encircled by which ligament?","choices":["The patellar ligament","The interosseous membrane","The anular ligament","The transverse scapular ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The head of the radius is held against the radial notch of the ulna by the anular ligament, which encircles it and allows rotation. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Where do the two pubic bones meet anteriorly?","choices":["The acetabulum","The obturator foramen","The pubic symphysis","The greater sciatic notch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pubic symphysis is the joint where the two pubic bones meet anteriorly, at the pubic crest. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which posterior depression of the distal humerus receives the olecranon during full extension of the elbow?","choices":["The coronoid fossa","The radial fossa","The intertubercular sulcus","The olecranon fossa"],"answer":3,"exp":"The olecranon fossa is the deep posterior depression of the distal humerus that accommodates the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow is fully extended. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How many carpal bones make up the wrist, and how are they arranged?","choices":["6 bones, arranged in a single continuous row","8 bones, arranged in a single row of eight","10 bones, arranged in two rows of five each","8 bones, in two rows of four (proximal and distal)"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are 8 carpal bones, arranged in two rows of four: a proximal row and a distal row. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which small notch on the superior border of the scapula transmits the suprascapular nerve?","choices":["The suprascapular (scapular) notch","The spinoglenoid notch of the scapula","The radial notch of the ulna","The trochlear notch of the ulna"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suprascapular notch on the superior border of the scapula transmits the suprascapular nerve beneath the transverse scapular ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"What is the interosseous membrane of the leg?","choices":["A membrane found only at the proximal ends of the tibia and fibula","A membrane spanning the entire length between the fibula and tibia","A membrane covering the tarsal bones","A ligament connecting the femur to the tibia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interosseous membrane of the leg spans the entire length between the fibula and tibia. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the function of the greater and lesser trochanters of the femur?","choices":["They articulate directly with the tibial plateau at the knee","They form part of the acetabulum on the coxal bone","They serve as distal landmarks that help form the knee joint","They serve as muscle attachment sites on the proximal femur"],"answer":3,"exp":"The greater and lesser trochanters are proximal femoral prominences that serve as attachment sites for hip and thigh muscles. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which two distal humeral landmarks articulate with the radius and ulna respectively?","choices":["Capitulum (radius) and trochlea (ulna)","Medial epicondyle (radius) and lateral epicondyle (ulna)","Trochlea (radius) and capitulum (ulna)","Olecranon fossa (radius) and coronoid fossa (ulna)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The capitulum articulates with the head of the radius, while the trochlea articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the acetabulum?","choices":["A stout ligament that binds the two pubic bones together anteriorly at the pubic symphysis","The wide opening bordered by the ischium and pubis that transmits the obturator nerve and vessels","The anterior fibrocartilaginous joint connecting the two coxal bones at the midline","The deep socket on the coxal bone where the ilium, ischium, and pubis converge, articulating with the femoral head"],"answer":3,"exp":"The acetabulum is the deep socket formed where the ilium, ischium, and pubis converge, and it articulates with the head of the femur at the hip joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is a coxal bone made of?","choices":["The femur and the acetabular rim","The ilium, ischium, and pubis","The pubis bone and the coccyx","The ilium bone and the sacrum only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each coxal (hip) bone is made of three fused bones: the ilium, ischium, and pubis. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is C7 called the vertebra prominens?","choices":["It bears the dens for skull rotation","It lacks a vertebral body entirely","It is fused into the sacral bone","Its long spinous process is easily felt"],"answer":3,"exp":"C7 is called the vertebra prominens because its long, non-bifid spinous process forms a prominent, easily palpable bump at the base of the neck. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What are fontanelles, and what is their function?","choices":["Small foramina present throughout the infant skull that transmit individual cranial nerves to the scalp","Membranous areas where sutures will eventually form, allowing distortion of the skull during childbirth","Fully ossified adult joints of the skull that form only well after skeletal maturity is reached","Permanent cartilage plates within the skull that are never expected to undergo ossification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fontanelles are membranous areas of the infant skull where sutures will eventually form; they allow the skull to distort somewhat during childbirth. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What structure of the ethmoid is perforated by small foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers?","choices":["Superior nasal concha","Ethmoidal labyrinth","Cribriform plate","Perpendicular plate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cribriform plate is perforated by small foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity to the brain. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which paired projections on the occipital bone articulate with the first cervical vertebra?","choices":["The mastoid processes","The occipital condyles","The styloid processes","The pterygoid processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The occipital condyles flank the foramen magnum and articulate with the superior facets of the atlas (C1). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which feature of thoracic vertebrae allows them to articulate with the ribs?","choices":["Transverse foramina in the processes","Costal facets (demifacets) on the body","A bifid, two-pronged spinous process","A completely missing vertebral body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thoracic vertebrae bear costal facets, often as demifacets, on their bodies and transverse processes for articulation with the ribs. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which suture separates the frontal bone from the two parietal bones?","choices":["The sagittal suture","The lambdoid suture","The squamous suture","The coronal suture"],"answer":3,"exp":"The coronal suture crosses the top of the skull, separating the anterior frontal bone from the parietal bones behind it. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are the three cranial fossae?","choices":["Anterior, middle, and posterior cranial fossa","Medial, lateral, and posterior cranial fossa","Superior, middle, and inferior cranial fossa","Anterior, lateral, and posterior cranial fossa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three cranial fossae are the anterior cranial fossa, middle cranial fossa, and posterior cranial fossa. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Through which opening of the frontal bone do vessels and a nerve reach the forehead above the eye?","choices":["The infraorbital foramen","The stylomastoid foramen","The supraorbital foramen","The greater palatine foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The supraorbital foramen (or notch) transmits the supraorbital vessels and nerve onto the forehead above the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of the vertebral column?","choices":["Serves as the body's exclusive, primary site for all red blood cell production, independent of other bones","Forms the main structural framework that is directly responsible for respiration and movement of the rib cage","Encloses and protects the spinal cord while supporting the skull and transferring weight to the lower limbs","Houses and protects the pituitary gland within a small, dedicated bony recess of the skull"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vertebral column encloses and protects the spinal cord, supports the skull and the weight of the head/neck/trunk, transfers weight to the lower limbs, and helps maintain upright posture. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which maxillary landmark contains the air-filled sinuses of the upper jaw?","choices":["Palatine processes","Alveolar processes","Infra-orbital groove","Maxillary sinuses"],"answer":3,"exp":"The maxillary sinuses are the air-filled chambers within the maxillae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Spinal nerves exit the vertebral column through which openings between adjacent vertebrae?","choices":["The intervertebral foramina","The vertebral foramina","The transverse foramina","The nutrient foramina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Spinal nerves pass out of the vertebral canal through the intervertebral foramina, the lateral openings between the pedicles of adjacent vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the atlas (C1) compared to typical vertebrae, and what movement does it allow?","choices":["It has a dens that projects superiorly from its body; it allows lateral flexion of the neck","It has no body and instead consists of anterior/posterior arches and tubercles; it allows the head to nod \"yes\"","It is fused directly to the axis below it, which allows essentially no independent movement","It has the largest vertebral body of any cervical vertebra; it allows the head to rotate \"no\""],"answer":1,"exp":"The atlas (C1) has no body, instead consisting of anterior and posterior vertebral arches and tubercles, and it articulates with the occipital condyles to allow the head to nod in a \"yes\" motion. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How many total bones make up the adult vertebral column, and how are they distributed?","choices":["33 bones: 24 vertebrae, 5 sacral, and 4 coccygeal, all unfused","26 bones: 26 individual vertebrae with no fused segments","24 bones: 24 vertebrae only, with no sacrum or coccyx counted","26 bones: 24 vertebrae, 1 sacrum, and 1 coccyx"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adult vertebral column is made up of 26 bones: 24 vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar), 1 sacrum (5 fused vertebrae), and 1 coccyx (3 to 5 fused vertebrae). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the lambdoid suture?","choices":["The suture between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones","The suture between the temporal bone and the parietal bone","The suture between the two parietal bones","The suture between the frontal bone and the two parietal bones"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lambdoid suture is the suture between the occipital bone and the two parietal bones. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The inferior opening at the end of the sacral canal, used for certain anesthetic injections, is called what?","choices":["The sacral hiatus","The greater sciatic notch","The obturator foramen","The intervertebral foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sacral hiatus is the opening at the inferior end of the sacral canal, formed where the laminae of the fifth sacral vertebra fail to fuse. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which extrinsic muscle produces flexion of toes 2 through 5?","choices":["Flexor digitorum longus","Flexor hallucis longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Extensor digitorum longus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Flexor digitorum longus produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which three muscles converge and insert at the medial tibial tubercle to form the pes anserine bursae?","choices":["Pectineus, gracilis, and adductor magnus","Vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and rectus femoris","Gracilis, sartorius, and semitendinosus","Adductor longus, adductor brevis, and adductor magnus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gracilis, sartorius, and semitendinosus converge and insert at the medial tibial tubercle to form the pes anserine bursae. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which broad muscle of the back extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm, and is called the 'swimmer's muscle'?","choices":["The trapezius muscle","The rhomboid major","The latissimus dorsi","The levator scapulae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The latissimus dorsi extends, adducts, and medially rotates the humerus and is nicknamed the swimmer's muscle. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes and abducts the wrist, named for its relationship to the radius?","choices":["Palmaris longus","Extensor carpi radialis longus","Flexor carpi radialis","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flexor carpi radialis flexes and abducts the wrist. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscles both adduct and produce downward rotation of the scapula?","choices":["Rhomboid major and rhomboid minor","Pectoralis minor and subclavius","Serratus anterior and subclavius","Trapezius and levator scapulae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Both the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor adduct the scapula and produce downward rotation of the scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"The muscles of the medial thigh compartment, such as the adductor longus and gracilis, primarily perform which action?","choices":["Abduction of the thigh","Adduction of the thigh","Dorsiflexion of the foot","Extension of the elbow"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial compartment muscles, including the adductor longus, brevis, magnus, and gracilis, primarily adduct the thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which intrinsic foot muscle produces flexion of toes 2 through 5 and is located in the sole of the foot?","choices":["Quadratus plantae","Extensor digitorum brevis","Flexor digitorum longus","Flexor digitorum brevis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Flexor digitorum brevis is an intrinsic muscle that produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"The posterior compartment of the leg, which plantarflexes the foot, is supplied by which nerve?","choices":["The deep fibular nerve","The tibial nerve","The superficial fibular nerve","The obturator nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tibial nerve supplies the posterior leg compartment, whose muscles plantarflex the foot and flex the toes. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which anterior chest muscle adducts, medially rotates, and flexes the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["The trapezius muscle","The rhomboid minor","The infraspinatus","The pectoralis major"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pectoralis major is the large anterior chest muscle that adducts, medially rotates, and flexes the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The hypothenar eminence is the fleshy mass at the base of which digit?","choices":["The thumb","The index finger","The middle finger","The little finger"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hypothenar eminence is the fleshy mass at the base of the little finger, formed by the hypothenar muscles. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which muscle produces extension, adduction, and medial rotation at the shoulder, and is a major muscle of the posterior thoraco-lumbar region?","choices":["Coracobrachialis","Latissimus dorsi","Supraspinatus","Subscapularis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Latissimus dorsi produces extension, adduction, and medial rotation at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The soleus and gastrocnemius are sometimes grouped together under which common name?","choices":["The quadriceps femoris","The rotator cuff","The triceps surae","The hamstring group"],"answer":2,"exp":"The gastrocnemius and soleus together are called the triceps surae, sharing the calcaneal tendon to plantarflex the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which of the four quadriceps muscles also flexes the thigh at the hip because it crosses two joints?","choices":["The vastus lateralis","The vastus medialis","The rectus femoris","The vastus intermedius"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rectus femoris crosses both the hip and knee, so besides extending the knee it also flexes the thigh at the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which hamstring muscle also produces extension at the hip, in addition to flexing the lower leg?","choices":["Semitendinosus","Popliteus","Biceps femoris","Semimembranosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Biceps femoris flexes the lower leg and also produces extension at the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which four-part anterior thigh muscle group is the prime extensor of the knee?","choices":["The quadriceps femoris","The hamstring group","The gluteal group","The adductor group"],"answer":0,"exp":"The quadriceps femoris, comprising the rectus femoris and three vasti, is the prime extensor of the knee. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"The serratus posterior superior and inferior muscles are considered accessory muscles of what?","choices":["Mastication","Eye movement","Swallowing","Respiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"The serratus posterior superior and inferior are thin muscles that assist respiration by acting on the ribs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"The main function of the pelvic floor (pelvic diaphragm) muscles is to do what?","choices":["Support the pelvic organs","Elevate the ribs in breathing","Rotate the forearm bones","Extend the vertebral column"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the bladder, rectum, and other pelvic organs and resists the downward pressure of the abdomen. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle turns the eye medially (adducts it) toward the nose?","choices":["The lateral rectus","The medial rectus","The superior oblique","The inferior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial rectus adducts the eye, turning the pupil toward the nose. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which muscle raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead, covering the top of the skull?","choices":["The occipitofrontalis","The orbicularis oris","The buccinator muscle","The masseter muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The occipitofrontalis (epicranius) covers the scalp and raises the eyebrows, wrinkling the forehead in surprise. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle group makes up the superficial layer of extrinsic back muscles?","choices":["Splenius capitis and splenius cervicis of the neck","The erector spinae muscle group by itself","Serratus posterior superior and inferior, deep to the rhomboids","Trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, and rhomboid muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superficial layer of extrinsic back muscles consists of the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, and rhomboid muscles — appendicular muscles that position the pectoral girdle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which large paired neck muscle flexes the neck and turns the head to the opposite side?","choices":["The sternocleidomastoid","The masseter muscle","The buccinator muscle","The diaphragm muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid flexes the neck and rotates the head to the opposite side; acting together the pair flex the neck. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Besides levator ani, which two additional muscles are listed as part of the anal triangle?","choices":["Deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter muscles","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus of the urogenital triangle","Obturator internus (lateral) and coccygeus, along with the external anal sphincter","Superficial transverse perineal muscle and the pelvic diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Besides levator ani, the anal triangle includes the (lateral) obturator internus, coccygeus, and the external anal sphincter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the erector spinae group, an example of the intermediate intrinsic back muscles?","choices":["Spinalis thoracis, longissimus thoracis, and iliocostalis lumborum","Longus capitis, longus colli, and quadratus lumborum","Multifidus, rotatores, and interspinales muscles","Splenius capitis, splenius cervicis, and semispinalis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The erector spinae group is made up of three muscles: spinalis thoracis (most medial), longissimus thoracis, and iliocostalis lumborum (most lateral). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"The erector spinae is composed of which three muscle columns from lateral to medial?","choices":["Trapezius, rhomboid, and levator","Iliocostalis, longissimus, and spinalis","Genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus","Masseter, temporalis, and pterygoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"From lateral to medial, the erector spinae comprises the iliocostalis, longissimus, and spinalis columns. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Into what four groups can the axial muscles be placed, based on location or function?","choices":["Superficial muscles, intermediate muscles, deep muscles, and intrinsic muscles, classified purely by their depth within the trunk and back","Muscles that produce flexion, muscles that produce extension, muscles that produce abduction, and muscles that produce adduction at each joint","Muscles of the head and neck, muscles of the vertebral column, muscles of the rib cage/abdominal-pelvic walls, and muscles of the pelvic floor","Muscles of the upper limb, muscles of the lower limb, muscles of the trunk wall, and muscles of the head region alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axial muscles can be placed into four groups based on location or function: muscles of the head and neck, muscles of the vertebral column, muscles of the rib cage and lateral walls of the abdominal/pelvic cavities, and muscles of the pelvic floor. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the action of the external intercostal muscles, and where do their fibers run?","choices":["Depress the ribs; same fiber direction as external intercostals","Compress the abdomen; unrelated to rib position","Depress the ribs; from the superior border of each rib to the inferior border of the rib above","Elevate the ribs; from the inferior border of each rib to the superior border of the rib below"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external intercostal muscles elevate the ribs, with fibers running from the inferior border of each rib to the superior border of the rib below. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Through which diaphragmatic opening does the esophagus pass?","choices":["The esophageal hiatus","The caval opening","The aortic hiatus","The obturator foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm at about the tenth thoracic level. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The combined contraction of the abdominal muscles raises intra-abdominal pressure to assist in what?","choices":["Rotating the eyeball outward","Forced expiration and coughing","Elevating the mandible","Flexing the wrist joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Contracting the abdominal wall raises intra-abdominal pressure to assist forced expiration, coughing, defecation, and childbirth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which muscle furrows the brow in the \"glabella\" area, pulling skin inferiorly and medially to wrinkle the brow?","choices":["Procerus","Levator palpebrae superioris","Corrugator supercilii","Orbicularis oculi"],"answer":2,"exp":"Corrugator supercilii furrows the brow in the glabella area, pulling skin inferiorly and medially to wrinkle the brow, innervated by CN VII. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where does the temporalis insert, after passing medial to the zygomatic arch?","choices":["The mental protuberance","The coronoid process of the mandible","The angle and lateral surface of the mandible","The medial surface of the mandibular ramus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The temporalis passes medial to the zygomatic arch to insert on the coronoid process of the mandible. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
